Calculation
Sum total_logins over the selected reporting period, grouped by application.






NoteExample queries






SQLR


SELECT
  application_name,
  SUM(total_logins) AS total_sso_events
FROM   ibm_verify.app_login_counts
-- set the reporting window; see the timespans note in cookbook/notes.qmd
WHERE  from_date BETWEEN DATE '2026-01-01' AND DATE '2026-05-28'
-- optionally filter out internal applications; see the internal applications
-- note in cookbook/notes.qmd
  AND application_name NOT IN (
      SELECT al.alias
      FROM   rp.alias al
      JOIN   rp.service s ON al.rp_id = s.rp_id
      WHERE  s.is_internal
  )
GROUP  BY application_name
ORDER  BY total_sso_events DESC;


internal_apps &lt;- tbl(con, in_schema("rp", "alias")) |&gt;
  inner_join(tbl(con, in_schema("rp", "service")), by = "rp_id") |&gt;
  filter(is_internal) |&gt;
  select(application_name = alias)

tbl(con, in_schema("ibm_verify", "app_login_counts")) |&gt;
  # set the reporting window; see the timespans note in cookbook/notes.qmd
  filter(
    from_date &gt;= as.Date("2026-01-01"),
    from_date &lt;= as.Date("2026-05-28")
  ) |&gt;
  # optionally filter out internal applications; see the internal applications
  # note in cookbook/notes.qmd
  anti_join(internal_apps, by = "application_name") |&gt;
  group_by(application_name) |&gt;
  summarise(
    total_sso_events = sum(total_logins, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(total_sso_events))

If you need a window we do not have
The Data and Research team cannot pull an ad hoc unique-user count for a custom date range. What they can do, given a legitimate business need, is add a new standing window to the pipeline so it is counted from then on. Before asking, know that:

adding a window takes weeks, not days or hours;
the new figure starts on the day it goes live, and nothing before that can be filled in;
the window must be 90 days or shorter, for the reasons above.

For the analyst-facing details, see the Cumulative Unique Users metric and the auth_total_logins catalog entry.
